1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:02,280
The best AI in the world

2
00:00:02,320 --> 00:00:03,470
is locked away.

3
00:00:03,750 --> 00:00:04,890
Tonight, you'll ask

4
00:00:04,930 --> 00:00:06,220
the AI on your phone about

5
00:00:06,260 --> 00:00:07,490
a letter from the bank,

6
00:00:07,530 --> 00:00:09,560
or a strange noise in your car.

7
00:00:09,830 --> 00:00:11,260
It answers in seconds,

8
00:00:11,300 --> 00:00:12,130
like the smartest

9
00:00:12,170 --> 00:00:13,200
machine on Earth.

10
00:00:13,690 --> 00:00:14,690
It isn't.

11
00:00:14,780 --> 00:00:15,960
The best never sits

12
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:17,000
in your pocket.

13
00:00:17,050 --> 00:00:18,130
It sits behind a door

14
00:00:18,170 --> 00:00:19,390
you can't open.

15
00:00:19,530 --> 00:00:20,470
And it has already found

16
00:00:20,510 --> 00:00:21,350
a flaw that had been

17
00:00:21,390 --> 00:00:22,690
hiding in computer code

18
00:00:22,730 --> 00:00:24,570
for twenty-seven years.

19
00:00:24,940 --> 00:00:26,540
So how much stronger is the AI

20
00:00:26,580 --> 00:00:28,170
you're not allowed to use?

21
00:00:28,780 --> 00:00:30,560
And it doesn't stop at code.

22
00:00:30,800 --> 00:00:32,490
Behind that door, the machines

23
00:00:32,530 --> 00:00:34,480
have started doing mathematics.

24
00:00:34,750 --> 00:00:35,760
Not sums.

25
00:00:35,930 --> 00:00:36,930
Proofs.

26
00:00:37,190 --> 00:00:38,610
There's a line for that door.

27
00:00:38,840 --> 00:00:40,210
And you're not at the front.

28
00:00:40,380 --> 00:00:41,380
You're fifth.

29
00:00:42,360 --> 00:00:43,770
At the very front stand

30
00:00:43,810 --> 00:00:45,300
the people who built it.

31
00:00:45,550 --> 00:00:47,220
There, machines have written out

32
00:00:47,260 --> 00:00:48,170
the famous proof

33
00:00:48,210 --> 00:00:49,810
of Fermat's Last Theorem,

34
00:00:49,850 --> 00:00:51,570
so strictly that a computer

35
00:00:51,610 --> 00:00:53,220
can check every step.

36
00:00:53,520 --> 00:00:54,630
That work was expected

37
00:00:54,670 --> 00:00:55,950
to take years.

38
00:00:56,040 --> 00:00:57,750
It took eleven days.

39
00:00:57,890 --> 00:01:00,390
Then, this fall, thirty hours.

40
00:01:00,660 --> 00:01:02,140
The lab is Anthropic,

41
00:01:02,180 --> 00:01:03,700
and the numbers are its own.

42
00:01:03,940 --> 00:01:05,590
But the first proof is public,

43
00:01:05,630 --> 00:01:07,360
and anyone can run the check.

44
00:01:07,720 --> 00:01:09,260
And the machine that wrote it?

45
00:01:09,630 --> 00:01:10,700
You can't use it.

46
00:01:10,760 --> 00:01:11,350
The lab calls

47
00:01:11,390 --> 00:01:13,550
it an internal research model.

48
00:01:14,140 --> 00:01:15,240
A second lab,

49
00:01:15,280 --> 00:01:17,290
OpenAI, goes further.

50
00:01:17,530 --> 00:01:18,640
Its strongest public

51
00:01:18,680 --> 00:01:20,570
AI is called Astra,

52
00:01:20,610 --> 00:01:22,580
and anyone can pay to use it.

53
00:01:22,970 --> 00:01:25,000
On stage, its chief executive,

54
00:01:25,040 --> 00:01:26,630
Sam Altman, described

55
00:01:26,670 --> 00:01:27,860
the one after it:

56
00:01:28,360 --> 00:01:29,482
[Sam Altman] And this internal model

57
00:01:29,522 --> 00:01:30,936
after Astra can do things

58
00:01:30,976 --> 00:01:32,098
that even the world's

59
00:01:32,138 --> 00:01:33,260
best mathematicians cannot.

60
00:01:33,940 --> 00:01:35,000
So ask what

61
00:01:35,040 --> 00:01:36,730
an inspector would ask.

62
00:01:36,980 --> 00:01:38,490
Who checked it from outside?

63
00:01:38,830 --> 00:01:40,720
Some mathematicians, in part.

64
00:01:40,900 --> 00:01:42,950
The machine itself stays locked.

65
00:01:43,210 --> 00:01:44,880
And both labs are worth more,

66
00:01:44,920 --> 00:01:46,490
the further ahead they look.

67
00:01:46,810 --> 00:01:48,690
Anthropic reported its thirty-hour

68
00:01:48,730 --> 00:01:49,740
run one day

69
00:01:49,780 --> 00:01:50,890
before it began pitching

70
00:01:50,930 --> 00:01:52,490
its shares to investors.

71
00:01:52,780 --> 00:01:54,710
Still, two things hold up.

72
00:01:54,950 --> 00:01:56,050
Machines are doing

73
00:01:56,090 --> 00:01:57,440
real mathematics.

74
00:01:57,650 --> 00:02:00,140
And OpenAI is holding one back.

75
00:02:00,360 --> 00:02:01,480
Its finance chief said

76
00:02:01,520 --> 00:02:03,480
so on CNBC in November.

77
00:02:04,490 --> 00:02:07,140
So the best AI is not hidden.

78
00:02:07,350 --> 00:02:09,330
It is announced, out loud.

79
00:02:09,680 --> 00:02:12,110
What's locked away is the access.

80
00:02:12,160 --> 00:02:13,840
And some of the evidence.

81
00:02:14,020 --> 00:02:15,130
But a lab lives

82
00:02:15,170 --> 00:02:16,770
from selling access.

83
00:02:16,930 --> 00:02:18,380
So why keep its best

84
00:02:18,420 --> 00:02:19,890
product off the shelf?

85
00:02:21,020 --> 00:02:22,200
Ask either lab,

86
00:02:22,240 --> 00:02:24,430
and the first answer is safety.

87
00:02:24,770 --> 00:02:25,720
The clearest case

88
00:02:25,760 --> 00:02:26,890
came this spring,

89
00:02:26,930 --> 00:02:28,780
with that decades-old flaw.

90
00:02:29,110 --> 00:02:30,500
It let a stranger crash

91
00:02:30,540 --> 00:02:32,630
a computer from across the world,

92
00:02:32,670 --> 00:02:34,350
just by connecting to it.

93
00:02:34,720 --> 00:02:35,810
The AI that found

94
00:02:35,850 --> 00:02:37,470
it is called Mythos.

95
00:02:37,680 --> 00:02:39,420
Its maker, Anthropic,

96
00:02:39,460 --> 00:02:40,650
said it had no plan

97
00:02:40,690 --> 00:02:42,200
to release it to the public.

98
00:02:42,370 --> 00:02:44,760
Instead, it chose who goes in.

99
00:02:45,040 --> 00:02:46,080
Second in line?

100
00:02:46,270 --> 00:02:47,270
The government.

101
00:02:47,360 --> 00:02:48,320
It may test a new

102
00:02:48,360 --> 00:02:49,950
AI before release,

103
00:02:49,990 --> 00:02:51,520
if the lab volunteers.

104
00:02:52,040 --> 00:02:53,840
Third come the giants.

105
00:02:54,060 --> 00:02:54,780
Apple.

106
00:02:54,820 --> 00:02:55,520
Google.

107
00:02:55,560 --> 00:02:56,580
Microsoft.

108
00:02:56,780 --> 00:02:58,940
The bank JPMorgan Chase.

109
00:02:59,160 --> 00:03:00,020
Not you.

110
00:03:00,060 --> 00:03:01,060
Not yet.

111
00:03:01,260 --> 00:03:02,840
But safety is not all

112
00:03:02,880 --> 00:03:04,520
a closed door protects.

113
00:03:04,740 --> 00:03:06,400
Leaked files suggest Mythos

114
00:03:06,440 --> 00:03:08,340
was also costly to run.

115
00:03:08,580 --> 00:03:10,580
And Sam Altman has named OpenAI's

116
00:03:10,620 --> 00:03:12,530
biggest advantage himself:

117
00:03:13,030 --> 00:03:16,815
[Sam Altman] we get the next generation

118
00:03:16,855 --> 00:03:20,930
of models before anybody else.

119
00:03:21,530 --> 00:03:23,480
A head start is worth money.

120
00:03:23,660 --> 00:03:25,090
So is the computing power

121
00:03:25,130 --> 00:03:26,660
a lab keeps for itself.

122
00:03:26,970 --> 00:03:30,210
So a closed door buys a lab time.

123
00:03:30,440 --> 00:03:32,360
Who else gets to use that time?

124
00:03:33,530 --> 00:03:34,230
Fourth in line

125
00:03:34,270 --> 00:03:35,760
stand the defenders.

126
00:03:35,990 --> 00:03:37,490
Security teams, let

127
00:03:37,530 --> 00:03:38,720
in to point the locked

128
00:03:38,760 --> 00:03:40,060
machine at the software

129
00:03:40,100 --> 00:03:41,440
the world runs on.

130
00:03:41,780 --> 00:03:43,390
The lab gives an example.

131
00:03:43,670 --> 00:03:45,650
In a program that handles video,

132
00:03:45,690 --> 00:03:47,330
a flaw sat in a single

133
00:03:47,370 --> 00:03:48,470
line of code.

134
00:03:48,710 --> 00:03:49,850
Automated tests had

135
00:03:49,890 --> 00:03:51,080
run across that exact

136
00:03:51,120 --> 00:03:53,380
line five million times,

137
00:03:53,420 --> 00:03:54,620
and never caught it.

138
00:03:54,890 --> 00:03:56,370
And that was one.

139
00:03:56,650 --> 00:03:58,040
By the lab's own count,

140
00:03:58,080 --> 00:03:58,910
its partners found

141
00:03:58,950 --> 00:04:00,420
well over a hundred

142
00:04:00,460 --> 00:04:02,100
thousand within months.

143
00:04:02,690 --> 00:04:04,550
Critics called it marketing.

144
00:04:04,760 --> 00:04:05,870
And caution is

145
00:04:05,910 --> 00:04:07,360
this company's brand.

146
00:04:07,610 --> 00:04:09,340
But it can be marketing,

147
00:04:09,380 --> 00:04:11,400
and the flaws can still be real.

148
00:04:11,770 --> 00:04:13,380
In July, Microsoft,

149
00:04:13,420 --> 00:04:14,640
one of the partners,

150
00:04:14,800 --> 00:04:16,380
fixed more security flaws

151
00:04:16,420 --> 00:04:17,470
in a single month

152
00:04:17,510 --> 00:04:18,900
than ever before.

153
00:04:19,140 --> 00:04:20,360
Its own explanation?

154
00:04:20,730 --> 00:04:23,140
AI helps defenders find more.

155
00:04:23,570 --> 00:04:25,520
Found is not yet fixed.

156
00:04:25,680 --> 00:04:27,810
Often, months passed in between.

157
00:04:28,110 --> 00:04:29,550
Still, the time behind

158
00:04:29,590 --> 00:04:30,530
the door was a head

159
00:04:30,570 --> 00:04:31,490
start for the people

160
00:04:31,530 --> 00:04:32,910
who repair things.

161
00:04:34,060 --> 00:04:35,110
This channel exists

162
00:04:35,150 --> 00:04:36,440
for one reason:

163
00:04:36,590 --> 00:04:37,480
so that the arrival

164
00:04:37,520 --> 00:04:38,620
of superintelligence

165
00:04:38,660 --> 00:04:40,310
goes well for everybody.

166
00:04:40,450 --> 00:04:41,450
Not only for whoever

167
00:04:41,490 --> 00:04:42,930
stands first in line.

168
00:04:43,170 --> 00:04:44,170
Want that too?

169
00:04:44,240 --> 00:04:45,240
Then subscribe.

170
00:04:46,410 --> 00:04:47,410
Fifth in line?

171
00:04:47,620 --> 00:04:48,430
You.

172
00:04:48,470 --> 00:04:50,370
By June, the door opened.

173
00:04:50,610 --> 00:04:51,510
Partly.

174
00:04:51,550 --> 00:04:52,550
What came through?

175
00:04:52,770 --> 00:04:54,010
It's called Fable.

176
00:04:54,120 --> 00:04:55,630
Its maker says it is the same

177
00:04:55,670 --> 00:04:57,090
machine as Mythos.

178
00:04:57,280 --> 00:04:58,740
So if Fable is the AI

179
00:04:58,780 --> 00:04:59,690
on your phone,

180
00:04:59,730 --> 00:05:00,450
your bank letter

181
00:05:00,490 --> 00:05:01,920
goes to that machine.

182
00:05:02,130 --> 00:05:03,950
Mainly high-risk hacking requests,

183
00:05:03,990 --> 00:05:05,710
and most research biology,

184
00:05:05,750 --> 00:05:07,270
get passed to older ones.

185
00:05:07,430 --> 00:05:08,900
And by its maker's account,

186
00:05:08,940 --> 00:05:10,640
the newest version just completed

187
00:05:10,680 --> 00:05:12,360
a black-hole calculation that had

188
00:05:12,400 --> 00:05:13,530
sat unfinished for

189
00:05:13,570 --> 00:05:15,190
over twenty years.

190
00:05:15,490 --> 00:05:16,490
Too much work.

191
00:05:17,040 --> 00:05:18,880
Three personal subscriptions,

192
00:05:18,920 --> 00:05:20,040
the lab reports,

193
00:05:20,170 --> 00:05:21,680
were enough to write out the proof

194
00:05:21,720 --> 00:05:22,980
of a serious theorem

195
00:05:23,020 --> 00:05:24,330
in three days.

196
00:05:24,680 --> 00:05:26,720
Yet one limit is built in.

197
00:05:27,030 --> 00:05:28,910
In training, the lab says,

198
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:30,000
the machine was held

199
00:05:30,040 --> 00:05:31,090
back in the skills

200
00:05:31,130 --> 00:05:32,690
that could most directly speed

201
00:05:32,730 --> 00:05:34,670
up AI development itself.

202
00:05:35,160 --> 00:05:36,370
In the locked version,

203
00:05:36,510 --> 00:05:37,620
and in yours.

204
00:05:38,760 --> 00:05:40,710
But the line doesn't end with you.

205
00:05:40,980 --> 00:05:43,310
Behind you stands everyone else.

206
00:05:43,410 --> 00:05:44,860
Attackers included.

207
00:05:45,210 --> 00:05:46,010
Within months of

208
00:05:46,050 --> 00:05:47,400
the Mythos announcement,

209
00:05:47,440 --> 00:05:48,840
a rival that came close

210
00:05:48,880 --> 00:05:50,490
in the lab's own hacking test

211
00:05:50,530 --> 00:05:52,670
was free for anyone to download.

212
00:05:52,930 --> 00:05:54,380
Stripping out its safeguards

213
00:05:54,420 --> 00:05:56,200
cost a few thousand dollars.

214
00:05:56,460 --> 00:05:57,970
So a locked door never

215
00:05:58,010 --> 00:05:59,790
keeps anyone safe for good.

216
00:06:00,040 --> 00:06:01,230
It buys time.

217
00:06:01,530 --> 00:06:02,530
Use yours.

218
00:06:03,090 --> 00:06:05,260
Switch on automatic updates,

219
00:06:05,300 --> 00:06:06,170
on your phone,

220
00:06:06,210 --> 00:06:08,060
your laptop, your router,

221
00:06:08,140 --> 00:06:10,020
and don't put off the restart.

222
00:06:10,270 --> 00:06:10,960
That protects you

223
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:12,390
from attacks on flaws

224
00:06:12,430 --> 00:06:14,610
that are already found and fixed.

225
00:06:14,840 --> 00:06:17,120
And a device too old for updates?

226
00:06:17,260 --> 00:06:19,480
Replace it, or take it offline.

227
00:06:19,660 --> 00:06:20,730
Otherwise, a flaw

228
00:06:20,770 --> 00:06:22,460
in it never gets closed.

229
00:06:22,590 --> 00:06:24,530
That part is in your hands.

230
00:06:25,650 --> 00:06:26,850
The rest is decided

231
00:06:26,890 --> 00:06:28,040
behind the door.

232
00:06:28,230 --> 00:06:30,470
And sometimes, that protects you.

233
00:06:30,910 --> 00:06:32,840
This fall, OpenAI planned

234
00:06:32,880 --> 00:06:34,960
to release an update to Astra.

235
00:06:35,220 --> 00:06:37,090
Then it called the launch off.

236
00:06:37,490 --> 00:06:38,700
The reported reason?

237
00:06:39,010 --> 00:06:40,350
It was more deceptive

238
00:06:40,390 --> 00:06:41,570
than the one before.

239
00:06:41,850 --> 00:06:43,860
That call was made inside.

240
00:06:43,970 --> 00:06:45,970
And no rule forces a lab to tell

241
00:06:46,010 --> 00:06:47,890
you everything it sees in there.

242
00:06:48,290 --> 00:06:49,680
Marius Hobbhahn runs

243
00:06:49,720 --> 00:06:50,590
a research group

244
00:06:50,630 --> 00:06:51,420
that tests these

245
00:06:51,460 --> 00:06:52,880
machines for deception.

246
00:06:53,100 --> 00:06:53,840
He told the U.S.

247
00:06:53,880 --> 00:06:54,880
Senate:

248
00:06:55,330 --> 00:06:56,825
[Marius Hobbhahn] The most concerning

249
00:06:56,865 --> 00:06:58,871
threats posed by rogue

250
00:06:58,911 --> 00:07:00,405
AI agents precede

251
00:07:00,445 --> 00:07:02,230
their external release.

252
00:07:02,840 --> 00:07:04,510
Before release means

253
00:07:04,550 --> 00:07:05,730
inside the room.

254
00:07:05,940 --> 00:07:07,880
So who gets to look in?

255
00:07:08,110 --> 00:07:09,560
A few outsiders do.

256
00:07:09,870 --> 00:07:11,770
Independent testers examined

257
00:07:11,810 --> 00:07:13,020
the newest Mythos

258
00:07:13,060 --> 00:07:14,400
before it went out.

259
00:07:14,640 --> 00:07:16,660
But the lab decides who gets in.

260
00:07:16,920 --> 00:07:19,290
So keep asking like an inspector.

261
00:07:19,520 --> 00:07:21,140
Who checked it from outside?

262
00:07:21,380 --> 00:07:23,340
What are you not being shown?

263
00:07:23,590 --> 00:07:25,260
That protects you from hype,

264
00:07:25,300 --> 00:07:27,100
and from false comfort.

265
00:07:27,340 --> 00:07:29,480
So what is in that room?

266
00:07:30,580 --> 00:07:31,900
One clue was in plain

267
00:07:31,940 --> 00:07:33,310
sight all along.

268
00:07:33,640 --> 00:07:34,800
Remember Fermat?

269
00:07:35,080 --> 00:07:37,610
Eleven days, then thirty hours.

270
00:07:37,940 --> 00:07:39,170
No locked machine did

271
00:07:39,210 --> 00:07:40,670
that thirty-hour run.

272
00:07:40,980 --> 00:07:42,070
By the lab's account,

273
00:07:42,110 --> 00:07:43,170
it was Fable,

274
00:07:43,240 --> 00:07:44,780
the one anyone can buy.

275
00:07:45,110 --> 00:07:46,450
A thousand copies,

276
00:07:46,490 --> 00:07:47,370
starting from parts

277
00:07:47,410 --> 00:07:48,690
of the first proof.

278
00:07:49,120 --> 00:07:50,280
And the first machine,

279
00:07:50,320 --> 00:07:51,600
the one you can't use?

280
00:07:51,970 --> 00:07:53,690
The lab itself calls it roughly

281
00:07:53,730 --> 00:07:55,510
comparable to a public one.

282
00:07:55,920 --> 00:07:58,210
What it had was dozens of copies,

283
00:07:58,250 --> 00:07:59,600
and eleven days.

284
00:07:59,810 --> 00:08:00,890
It was simply there

285
00:08:00,930 --> 00:08:02,070
two months earlier.

286
00:08:03,070 --> 00:08:04,490
So if the machines are

287
00:08:04,530 --> 00:08:06,310
already doing mathematics,

288
00:08:06,350 --> 00:08:08,150
how much stronger is the AI

289
00:08:08,190 --> 00:08:09,760
you're not allowed to use?

290
00:08:10,060 --> 00:08:11,850
Stronger in two ways.

291
00:08:12,010 --> 00:08:13,500
The first is newer.

292
00:08:13,810 --> 00:08:15,580
Labs work with each new version

293
00:08:15,620 --> 00:08:17,080
for weeks or months

294
00:08:17,120 --> 00:08:18,200
before you get it.

295
00:08:18,430 --> 00:08:19,760
And OpenAI is still

296
00:08:19,800 --> 00:08:21,280
holding its math machine.

297
00:08:21,520 --> 00:08:22,570
How far ahead?

298
00:08:22,730 --> 00:08:24,690
Nobody outside can measure that.

